[Desktop Entry]
Type=Application
Name=Big Screen
GenericName=Ten-Foot Interface
Comment=Your Steam library, Big Picture and the machine's own switches as tiles, on a television, driven with a game controller
# ⚠ --detach, and it is not optional here. Everything that launches a .desktop
# on this desktop is quickshell — the start menu, the dock, the widget launcher
# — and a child started from one inherits pipes quickshell closes as soon as its
# direct child exits. Without the fork this entry starts a full-screen shell
# that dies the moment the menu that launched it finishes.
#
# The synuirc `autostart =` line that `syn-arcade big autostart on` writes does
# NOT use this file and does NOT pass --detach: synui spawns it to be exactly
# that process, and exec-in-place is one process instead of two.
Exec=syn-arcade big start --detach
# Its own icon, not a stock theme name: a generic one makes the entry
# indistinguishable from whatever else in the menu wears the same face.
Icon=syn-arcade
Terminal=false
# Deliberately NO StartupWMClass. Big screen mode is a layer-shell surface, not
# a toplevel window — there is no window for a taskbar to match against, which
# is also why it never appears in the window list or the alt-tab ring.
Categories=Game;
Keywords=big;screen;picture;tv;television;couch;controller;gamepad;steam;bigpicture;launcher;arcade;

# Big screen mode is two quickshell PanelWindows — wlr-layer-shell surfaces —
# and layer-shell is a wlroots protocol. mutter has never implemented it, so
# under GNOME this entry started a shell that mapped nothing and exited: an
# icon in the app grid that does nothing when clicked, with no error anywhere.
# Confirmed on a GNOME session, 2026-08-18.
#
# KWin DOES implement layer-shell, so the surfaces would map under KDE — and
# they would then misbehave, because the tile hand-off and the fullscreen
# toggle both go through `synctl dispatch` and nothing answers it outside
# synui. Mapping and misbehaving is worse than not appearing, so this is
# OnlyShowIn rather than NotShowIn=GNOME.
#
# BOTH names, because the desktop identity depends on how the session was
# started: greetd exports synui, a display manager reading the session file
# exports SynapseOS. Naming one hides the entry on the other login path.
#
# ⚠ desktop-file-validate will ask for an X- prefix on these. Ignoring it is
# correct: OnlyShowIn is compared LITERALLY against the entries in
# XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP, so X-SynapseOS matches nothing and would hide this
# everywhere, which is the opposite of the intent.
#
# The Arcade entry beside this one is deliberately NOT gated. It is an
# ordinary FloatingWindow and most of what it configures — MangoHud, pads,
# deadzones — has nothing to do with the compositor. It was tested working
# under GNOME on the same day.
OnlyShowIn=synui;SynapseOS;
